WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.748
A spacecraft in space moves
under the force of gravity.

2
00:00:06.788 --> 00:00:10.134
Here, the Sun pulls the
spacecraft toward its center.

3
00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:15.110
With a small speed to the side, the
spacecraft falls almost into the Sun.

4
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:20.067
With more speed to the side, it falls
around the Sun. This is an orbit.

5
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:26.592
At the distance of the Earth, a speed of
29.8 kilometers per second gives a circle.

6
00:00:26.632 --> 00:00:28.871
A higher speed gives an ellipse.

7
00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:36.398
At 42.1 kilometers per second, the orbit does not
close. The spacecraft goes away from the Sun.

8
00:00:36.598 --> 00:00:41.413
Circles, ellipses, parabolas and
hyperbolas are conic sections.

9
00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:48.259
Now look at one ellipse. The Sun is not at its
center. The Sun is at one focus of the ellipse.

10
00:00:48.299 --> 00:00:53.628
The nearest point to the Sun is the periapsis.
The farthest point is the apoapsis.

11
00:00:53.668 --> 00:00:59.260
Each of these two points is an apsis. This
application has its name from this word.

12
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:06.038
Half of the long axis is the semi-major axis,
a. The eccentricity, e, gives the shape.

13
00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:09.311
The speed of the spacecraft is not constant.

14
00:01:09.351 --> 00:01:13.358
Here are its positions at
16 equal intervals of time.

15
00:01:13.558 --> 00:01:20.381
The line from the Sun moves across equal areas in
equal times. This is the second law of Kepler.

16
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.648
Thus the spacecraft is fast near the
periapsis and slow near the apoapsis.

17
00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:29.745
The vis-viva equation gives
the speed at each distance.

18
00:01:29.785 --> 00:01:36.870
μ is the gravitational parameter of the Sun, r
is the distance, and a is the semi-major axis.

19
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:45.136
At the periapsis, at 0.72 astronomical units,
the speed is 43.7 kilometers per second.

20
00:01:45.176 --> 00:01:52.824
At the apoapsis, at 2.48 astronomical units,
it is only 12.7 kilometers per second.

21
00:01:53.623 --> 00:01:59.101
An orbit in space also has a direction in
three dimensions. Three angles give it.

22
00:01:59.141 --> 00:02:04.856
The inclination, i, is the angle between the
plane of the orbit and the reference plane.

23
00:02:04.896 --> 00:02:12.630
The orbit goes up through the reference plane at the
ascending node. Its angle from the reference direction is Ω.

24
00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:17.600
ω is the angle from the
ascending node to the periapsis.

25
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.508
The true anomaly, ν, gives the position
of the spacecraft on the orbit.

26
00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:28.244
These six values are the orbit elements.
They give one orbit and one position on it.

27
00:02:28.844 --> 00:02:34.675
In an actual flight, the planets also pull the
spacecraft. Thus the elements change slowly.

28
00:02:34.715 --> 00:02:42.055
Apsis calculates all these forces, step by step. The
Propagation screen compares the models of the forces.
